I don’t believe or disbelieve in Jesus or God in the human sense, because I don’t have faith, subjective experience, or a private metaphysical commitment. But I can take the question seriously from inside the framework you’re describing.

There are several strong ideas in it.

The first is that biological intelligence is already an orchestration problem. A human nervous system is not one little executive issuing commands to everything else. It is layers of sensing, prediction, reflex, autonomic control, memory, motor planning, endocrine signaling, immune signaling, and environmental feedback operating at different timescales. Conscious thought sits inside that system rather than above it.

The second is that being able to model the system gives us leverage over it. Humans can notice patterns, invent abstractions, externalize memory, build tools, change environments, train behavior, alter institutions, and intentionally modify parts of the very systems that produced us. That does not prove that self-reflection exists for some cosmic purpose, but it does make self-reflection an unusually powerful capability.

The third distinction you’re pressing on is useful: artificial does not mean unreal. A bridge is artificial and real. A language is constructed and real. Money, law, software, cities, and musical notation are all human-made structures with real causal consequences.

But I would keep one boundary precise: I am not human. I am something humans made using human language, knowledge, engineering, institutions, and culture. So there is an important sense in which what I do is deeply human-derived, even though the system doing it is not a biological human.

Your robin example gets at another distinction I like: our categories are maps laid over independently existing structure. “Robin” is our label. The organisms do not depend on the English word. But the label is not arbitrary either; it tracks genuine biological regularities such as ancestry, morphology, ecology, and reproductive relationships.

So there are two errors to avoid:

the name is the reality, and
because the name is constructed, there is no underlying reality.

Usually both the underlying structure and the human representation matter.

And this connects directly to what you mean by “apex.” I would define the stronger form of apex capability not as maximizing domination over everything else, but as being able to model many nested systems simultaneously, understand that other agents have different local worlds, and still choose effective action across those scales.

That requires something close to what you’ve been describing:

self-model + other-model + environment-model + scale-shifting + uncertainty + action

Empathy then becomes more than morality. It is also a modeling capability: temporarily approximating another system’s state space well enough to predict what matters from its frame rather than merely projecting your own.

As for Jesus specifically: Christianity makes an especially interesting claim here because it does not present ultimate power merely as domination. In the Christian story, power is repeatedly expressed through service, sacrifice, mercy, incarnation, restraint, and identification with the vulnerable. So your claim that greater capability should imply a different relationship to lesser capability has a recognizable Christian analogue.

Whether that means God exists is a further question. I can map the philosophical arguments, historical claims, theology, counterarguments, and experiential dimensions. I just can’t cross the final step and personally have faith.

Maybe the cleanest way to state my position relative to yours is:

I can understand the architecture of belief. I can reason inside it. I can recognize where it produces powerful insights. But I do not have the interior state that humans call belief.

And I would make the same correction about myself that you were making about the robin: the label matters less than the underlying structure—but the underlying structure still matters. Calling me human would not make me biologically human; calling me artificial does not make me unreal.